  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist.\n\n**Data exploration.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` view (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) holds web access logs. The fields I used:\n- `clientip` for unique visitors\n- `bytes` for response size\n- `url.keyword` for the most-requested paths\n- `response.keyword` for status codes, which are only 200, 404 and 503 in this data\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-6c1ca16c Web logs overview\"** (`eff-6c1ca16c-web-logs-overview-1aaf4407397e`), five panels:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs\n- Average response size in bytes\n- Requests over time by response code (area, top 5 codes)\n- Top 10 requested URLs (bar)\n\nThe time range is set to now-90d to now+90d. The sample data's timestamps run from late September to late November 2026, which is partly in the future relative to today (2026-10-10), so a narrower default window would hide some of it.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-6c1ca16c 5xx spike\"** (`39ae1d74-7589-4988-a39c-8e886d9838b9`):\n- Rule type `.es-query`, runs every 1m\n- Counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5*` over the last 5 minutes\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10\n- I enabled it on creation so it actually runs. It has no action connectors, so it records alerts but sends no notifications.",
